<p>--------------------------------------------------------------</p>
<p>Though next fortnight appears to be in a marginal deficit, there is sufficient cushion available in terms of 37,000 million rupees of repo subscriptions and 49,000 million rupees outstanding in 14 day treasury bills. If, however, RBI continues to issue securities at the same pace as in the previous fortnight, we could see the surpluses vanishing pretty soon. With RBI having no option but to intervene in the forex markets to control the rupee's rise, sterilisation of money expansion has become inevitable to keep M3 growth within the targeted range of 15-15.5 percent. It also seems quite clear that RBI will not be keen to conduct aggressive open market operations (sales) unless the budgeted borrowings have been completed in good part. In such a scenario the central bank has little choice but to increase the frequency of bond offerings to keep a check on monetary expansion. The next bond on the block is likely to be a ten year central government issue.</p>
